## Staggering Numbers in Food Safety Failures

June 2026 was a month to remember--unfortunately, for all the wrong reasons. With a record 212 food recalls, it overtook May's previous high of 173, signaling a troubling trend in food safety compliance for the year.

### The Repeat Offender Club
Among the 94 repeat offenders identified so far in 2026, Spring & Mulberry Inc. and MOGO Moringa LLC stand out for their consistent appearances in recall notices. Spring & Mulberry Inc., with a score of 96 and a grade of 'F,' had 11 Class I recalls--these involve situations where there's a reasonable probability that the use of a violative product will cause serious adverse health consequences or death.

MOGO Moringa LLC's recent recall on July 8 due to Salmonella contamination in their herbal supplement capsules only underscores the critical need for proactive safety measures. Salmonella Typhimurium contamination, as seen in this case, can lead to severe infections, especially in vulnerable populations.

### Top Reasons for Recalls
The leading causes for recalls this year include allergens (301 instances), Salmonella (197), and foreign objects (100). Notably, the prevalence of allergens and pathogens highlights gaps in both labeling practices and manufacturing hygiene.

### Recent Recalls: A Closer Look
1. **NARA ORGANICS INC** recalled their infant formula due to potential Clostridium botulinum contamination. Infant formula failures are particularly concerning due to the vulnerable population they serve.
2. **Western Mixers Produce & Nuts, Inc.** faced issues with undeclared peanuts in their dark chocolate raisins, posing a significant risk to individuals with peanut allergies. 

### Brands Under Scrutiny
Other brands with poor track records include Moonlight Packing Corporation, Ghirardelli Chocolate Company, and Diva Fam Inc. The latter two have received 'F' grades and scored in the mid-to-high 80s based on the frequency and severity of their recalls.

## Call to Action
While regulatory agencies continue to enforce safety standards, consumers remain their last line of defense. Being informed about recent recalls and avoiding products from high-risk brands is not just advisable--it could be lifesaving.
